 The backend process also runs `@nestjs/schedule` crons in-process — including **`wa-housekeeping`** (every 15 min): auto-resolves idle WhatsApp conversations per their team's `autoResolveAfterDays`, un-snoozes due ones, purges `wa_webhook_events` + orphan `wa_message_status_events` older than 30 days, and (WP4 Half B) purges `wa_ai_tool_calls`, `wa_ai_runs` and `wa_automation_runs` older than 24 months. The purge is a PII retention promise (raw Meta bodies carry phone numbers) — if it is disabled, the real-number parallel run must not start. No env var; the team settings are the only knobs.
 
 - **`wa-sla`** (every minute, in the backend process): flags first-response breaches and escalates them to team managers (in-app + email). Business-hours aware per team; nothing to configure but the team's SLA minutes and business hours on `/admin/whatsapp/settings/teams`. If the backend is down for an hour, the tick simply re-derives everything from the database on the next run — nothing is carried between ticks.
 - **`wa-template-sync`** (daily 03:00, in the backend process): pulls every ACTIVE WhatsApp account's templates from Meta. Webhooks keep the registry fresh in real time; this catches what they cannot — an event missed while the app was down, and a template created or edited directly in Meta's Business Manager. Stateless, per-account try/catch, no env var. A quiet log line means nothing drifted.
 - **`wa-media`** (Bull queue, backend process): retries inbound WhatsApp media downloads up to 5 times with exponential backoff, then marks the row FAILED so the thread says the attachment is unavailable. Visible in the admin queue monitor (`GET /api/admin/queues`, super-admin only) beside `wa-inbound` and `wa-outbound`. No env var. A backlog here means Meta or MinIO is unreachable — the messages themselves are unaffected.
 - **`careers-retention`** (daily 03:00 UTC) also anonymises the `wa_*` rows of contacts linked only to the applicants it anonymises, and DELETES their WhatsApp media objects from MinIO. It is the only sanctioned deleter of those objects; a restore from backup is the only way back.
+- **Website crons** (since WP3a, in the backend process; each one begins its tick with `websiteModuleEnabled(config)` and does nothing while `WEBSITE_MODULE_ENABLED` is not `true`): **`website-alarms`** (every 5 min) announces a form's untrip once its 60-minute abuse window has expired and raises `WEBSITE_FORMS_UNAVAILABLE(reason: drought)` — once per company day, only inside Europe/Istanbul business hours — when no real (non-test) submission arrived for 48 hours; **`website-purge`** (daily **04:30**, container clock — 03:00 is `careers-retention`'s slot) deletes `website_form_submissions` rows past `purgeAfter` (90 days after HANDLED) including the `website-fraud/` MinIO objects they name — the two new PII tables are outside `careers-retention`, so their purge lives here. No env var beyond the flag; the numbers (trip threshold 25, window 3600 s, drought 48 h, business hours) are constants in `modules/website/website-alarms.logic.ts`, code not config, and the once-per-window markers are Redis keys (`website:alarm:*`, `website:abuse:*`) that expire on their own.
 
 **A new workspace package must be added to FOUR Dockerfiles, by hand (2026-09-15).** `packages/permissions` (the shared permission resolver, read by both the backend and the frontend) needed a `COPY packages/<name>/package.json` line in `apps/backend/Dockerfile`, in BOTH stages of `apps/backend/Dockerfile.prod`, and the same in the two frontend Dockerfiles — plus an explicit `RUN npm run build --workspace=packages/<name>` in each `.prod` file, which lists its package builds one per line rather than globbing. Miss the build line and the container starts but the seed dies on `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`, because `prisma/role-defaults.ts` re-exports the package and the seed resolves it through `main: ./dist/index.js`. The dev Dockerfiles glob (`npx turbo run build --filter='./packages/*'`) and so only need the manifest COPY. **This failure is invisible until a production deploy**, which is why it is written down here rather than left to be rediscovered.
 
 **Storage is MinIO in production** (not S3). `MINIO_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=files.operations.jobsadmire.com` is the public URL the backend signs/serves against. The `AWS_*` variables present in `.env` are **dead legacy** — nothing in the backend reads them (`upload.service.ts` uses only `MINIO_*`).
 
